VOCAB = [
 ('Allies', 'The countries that fought Germany and its partners: chiefly France, Britain and its empire, Russia (until 1917), Italy (from 1915), and the United States (from 1917), with Belgium, Serbia, and many others.'),
 ('Central Powers', 'Germany, Austria-Hungary, the Ottoman Empire, and Bulgaria.'),
 ('Western Front', 'The line of trenches across Belgium and France, about 450 miles from the North Sea to Switzerland.'),
 ('Eastern Front', 'The much longer and more open front between Germany and Austria-Hungary on one side and Russia on the other.'),
 ('Mobilization', 'Calling up a country\'s trained reserves and moving its armies to the border. In 1914 it took millions of men and thousands of trains, and once begun it was very hard to stop.'),
 ('Neutral', 'Taking no side in a war. Belgium\'s neutrality was guaranteed by a treaty Germany had signed.'),
 ('Trench', 'A deep ditch soldiers dug for protection from bullets and shells. By 1915 each side had several lines of them, with barbed wire in front.'),
 ('No man\'s land', 'The strip of ground between the two sides\' front trenches, sometimes only fifty yards wide.'),
 ('Over the top', 'Climbing out of a trench to attack across no man\'s land.'),
 ('Artillery barrage', 'A storm of shells from cannon, fired to destroy defenses or to keep defenders\' heads down while infantry advanced.'),
 ('Stalemate', 'A situation in which neither side can win or move. The Western Front barely moved from late 1914 to early 1918.'),
 ('Attrition', 'Trying to win by killing and wounding so many of the enemy that he runs out of men first.'),
 ('Poison gas', 'Chlorine, phosgene, and mustard gas, released from cylinders or fired in shells. It blinded, burned, and choked.'),
 ('Salient', 'A bulge in the front line that sticks out into enemy territory and can be fired on from three sides.'),
 ('U-boat', 'A German submarine, from the German word Unterseeboot.'),
 ('Convoy', 'A group of cargo ships sailing together under the protection of warships.'),
 ('Blockade', 'Using warships to stop food and supplies from reaching an enemy country by sea.'),
 ('Tank', 'An armored vehicle on tracks, invented in Britain to cross trenches and crush barbed wire. The name was a cover story: the first ones were shipped as "water tanks."'),
 ('Doughboy', 'Nickname for an American soldier in this war.'),
 ('Shell shock', 'The name given at the time to the mental injuries of soldiers who had been under bombardment.'),
 ('Propaganda', 'Information, true or false, spread to win support for a cause. Every government in this war censored the news and shaped it.'),
 ('Genocide', 'The deliberate destruction of a people. The word was invented in 1944 by a lawyer who had studied what happened to the Armenians.'),
 ('Armistice', 'An agreement to stop fighting. The armistice of November 11, 1918, ended the fighting; the peace treaty was signed at Versailles in June 1919.'),
 ('Fourteen Points', 'President Wilson\'s plan for a peace without revenge, including freedom of the seas, self-government for Europe\'s peoples, and a League of Nations.')
]
ADVISORY = [
 'This atlas includes the Armenian Genocide. The entry describes mass killing and deportation in the words of the American ambassador who reported it. Preview it, and plan time to talk.',
 'The entries on the Second Battle of Ypres and Passchendaele describe men killed by poison gas and drowned in mud.',
 'The numbers in this war are hard to take in: about 9 million soldiers and several million civilians died. Casualty figures for single battles are estimates, and for the Eastern Front they are very rough.',
 'Most of the sources here were printed during the war or just after it. Newspaper reports passed through military censors, and generals\' memoirs were written to defend their decisions. The first-day report from the Somme is included partly to show how far the news could be from what happened.',
 'Writers of the time used words for the enemy, such as "Boche" and "Hun," that were meant as insults. One quotation leaves out a sergeant\'s swearing and says so.',
 'The period map shows the borders of 1914. Poland, Czechoslovakia, Yugoslavia, Finland, and the Baltic states did not yet exist, and Ireland was part of the United Kingdom.',
 'This atlas covers Europe and the nearest part of the Ottoman Empire. The war was also fought in Africa, the Middle East, and Asia, and soldiers and laborers from India, Africa, the Caribbean, China, and Indochina served on the Western Front.'
]
